<BaseLayout title="CDLR: Chain Driven Live Roller Conveyors | Heavy-Duty Guide" description="CDLR (chain driven live roller) for heavy pallets and cases. Capacity per foot, zoned accumulation, chain types, and CDLR vs MDR vs belt-driven." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>CDLR: Chain Driven Live Roller Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A CDLR (Chain Driven Live Roller) conveyor uses roller chain and sprockets to positively drive conveyor rollers, providing heavy-duty transport with no slip for pallets, drums, heavy cases, and other unit loads. CDLR is the standard powered conveyor technology for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet handling systems</a> and end-of-line packaging, and for any load that exceeds the rating of belt-driven or <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR roller conveyors</a>. Capacity is rated in pounds per linear foot, so the support spacing and roller size you specify set what the conveyor can carry.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How CDLR Conveyors Work</h2>
<p>Each roller in a CDLR conveyor carries sprockets at one end. Roller chain links the sprockets, and a gearmotor drives the chain, which positively rotates every roller in the section. Rollers turn regardless of product weight, surface friction, or load distribution.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/cdlr-hytrol-pallet-conveyor.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="A strapped bulk carton on a plastic pallet sits on a blue roller conveyor with steel rollers, with gearmotors mounted along the side frame." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A palletized load on a Hytrol chain driven live roller conveyor. Gearmotors are mounted along the side frame. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/pallet-handling/chain-roller-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Chain Drive Configurations</h3>
<p>CDLR conveyors use two chain drive configurations. In the <strong>roll-to-roll</strong> (paired loop) configuration, short chain loops connect each roller to the next, so drive passes from roller to roller down the section. Each loop can be replaced without dismantling the whole drive. In the <strong>continuous chain</strong> configuration, a single chain runs along the section and engages a sprocket on each roller.</p>
<p>Chain size rises with load and roller pitch. In Hytrol's line, the 199-CRR uses #50 chain, the <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/category/3251/26-crr-heavy-duty-straight-chain-driven-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">26-CRR</a> uses #40 chain on 4-inch roller centers, #50 chain on 3, 3-3/4, 5, 7-1/2, or 10-inch centers, and #60 chain on 3, 3-3/4, 6, 7-1/2, or 12-inch centers, and the 36-CRRH uses #60 chain. ANSI chain numbers give pitch in eighths of an inch for the first digit: #40 is 1/2-inch pitch, #50 is 5/8-inch, and #60 is 3/4-inch. Chain guards protect operators and keep debris out of the chain path.</p>
<h3>Roller Construction</h3>
<p>CDLR rollers are heavier than standard package or <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> designs. Hytrol's examples: 2-1/8-inch by 1/8-inch wall rollers on the 199-CRR, 2-5/8-inch by 7-gauge rollers on the 26-CRR, and 3-1/2-inch by 0.300-inch wall rollers on the <a href="https://acgconveyors.com/partners/hytrol/chain-driven-live-roller-conveyors/36-crrh-heavy-duty-chain-driven-live-roller/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">36-CRRH</a>. Roller centers are chosen so the pallet or container is always supported across enough rollers, and offered centers depend on the model and chain size.</p>
<h3>Frame Construction</h3>
<p>CDLR frames use heavier steel than standard roller conveyor frames, and bed width is set by the pallet or container width plus clearance. Hytrol offers the 26-CRR in bed widths from 22-1/4 to 70-1/4 inches. Support spacing matters as much as frame gauge: the same 26-CRR is rated at 300 lbs per foot on 10-foot support centers and 1,000 lbs per foot on 5-foot centers.</p>
<h3>Speed and Control</h3>
<p>CDLR speed is set by gearmotor ratio and roller sprocket size, and pallet lines generally run slower than case lines. <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">Variable frequency drives</a> (VFDs) provide adjustable speed. CDLR sections integrate with PLC controls for start/stop sequencing, photo-eye product detection, and coordination with upstream and downstream equipment.</p>
<h3>Accumulation</h3>
<p>A continuous CDLR section accumulates with full backpressure unless stops are added. Zoned CDLR avoids this. <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/pallet-handling/chain-roller-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol</a> describes its chain driven conveyors as transporting or accumulating pallets, and its <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2015_661_crezd.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CREZD models</a> split the conveyor into zones, each with a zone controller, a zone starter box, and its own gearmotor. When a load reaches an occupied zone, that zone stops driving and signals the zone upstream, so pallets queue without touching and without compressed air.</p>
<h2 id="when-heading">When to Use CDLR vs Other Roller Types</h2>
<p>CDLR occupies the heavy-duty end of the <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> range. Choose CDLR over other roller conveyor types in these scenarios:</p>
<p><strong>Load exceeds lighter conveyor ratings.</strong> Loaded pallets, drums, and heavy containers usually exceed the per-zone rating of <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> and the rating of <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">belt-driven live roller</a>. Compare your heaviest load per foot against each quoted model.</p>
<p><strong>No slip is acceptable.</strong> Belt-driven and O-ring-driven roller conveyors rely on friction to transfer drive force. Heavy loads, wet surfaces, or dusty environments can cause slip. Chain-and-sprocket engagement provides positive drive regardless of surface conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Poor product bottom surface.</strong> Products with uneven, wet, or low-friction bottoms may not grip belt-driven rollers. CDLR rollers turn whether or not the product slips on them.</p>
<p><strong>Choose MDR or BDLR instead when:</strong> products are lighter cases, cartons, or totes within those conveyors' ratings, you want the simpler zone control of MDR, or noise and energy use of a continuous chain drive are concerns. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyor comparison</a> for a head-to-head analysis.</p>
